ak-venc: what this driver answers, and what it does not
=======================================================

Stage one - platform resources, clock and reset, the m2m job path, the two
queues and a fixed-QP IDR - has passed its hardware gate: one IDR byte-for-byte
identical to the lib/video userspace reference.

Stage two is IPPP with a rate controller: P frames, the GOP, the reference
rotation, frame_num and idr_pic_id, the frame-level bit budget, the MAD loop,
the core's checkpoint ladder and the standard control set. None of stage two
has run on hardware; sections 4 and 5 list what only a board can settle.

Stage three adds JPEG on the CAPTURE queue - section 6. It is additive: no
H.264 path changed, and a JPEG frame touches none of the H.264 session's
state.

1. uAPI: the stateful encoder contract
--------------------------------------

The formal "V4L2 Stateful Video Encoder Interface" postdates this kernel, so
full conformance to it is not reachable here - V4L2_BUF_FLAG_LAST does not
exist in include/linux/videodev2.h on 3.4.113 and neither does
V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_M2M. What follows is the contract as it stands, and what this
driver does about each item.

Answered:

  VIDIOC_QUERYCAP            VIDEO_CAPTURE | VIDEO_OUTPUT | STREAMING.
                             No VIDEO_M2M: the flag is not in this tree.
  VIDIOC_ENUM_FMT            both queues. OUTPUT: YU12 only. CAPTURE: H264
                             and JPEG.
  VIDIOC_G_FMT               both queues, valid before any S_FMT
                             (1280x720 I420 in, H.264 out).
  VIDIOC_TRY_FMT             both queues. Width is clamped to 1280 and to a
                             multiple of 16; height is NOT rounded up to 16,
                             because the core codes ALIGN(height,16) rows and
                             declares the difference in the bottom-edge
                             overfill field. 640x360 stays 640x360.
  VIDIOC_S_FMT               both queues, -EBUSY while the queue has buffers.
                             S_FMT(OUTPUT) sets the coded size; G_FMT(CAPTURE)
                             reports it.
  VIDIOC_REQBUFS             both queues, MMAP and USERPTR.
  VIDIOC_QUERYBUF            both queues.
  VIDIOC_QBUF / DQBUF        both queues.
  VIDIOC_STREAMON / _OFF     both queues.
  VIDIOC_G_PARM / S_PARM     OUTPUT only, timeperframe. The frame rate is what
                             the bit budget is denominated in. S_PARM also
                             turns on the SPS's VUI timing; without it the SPS
                             is byte-identical to stage one's.
  VIDIOC_QUERYCTRL           through the per-fh control handler.
  VIDIOC_G_CTRL / S_CTRL     through the per-fh control handler.
  VIDIOC_G_EXT_CTRLS /
    S_EXT_CTRLS / TRY_EXT_CTRLS / QUERYMENU
                             through the per-fh control handler.
  poll(), mmap()             v4l2_m2m_poll / v4l2_m2m_mmap.

Not answered - each one is a gap, not a decision that it is unnecessary:

  VIDIOC_ENCODER_CMD         V4L2_ENC_CMD_STOP / _START. The ioctl and the
  VIDIOC_TRY_ENCODER_CMD     command constants exist on this tree
                             (include/linux/videodev2.h, include/media/
                             v4l2-ioctl.h). Draining is stage two: the driver
                             has no drain state and does not emit an empty
                             final CAPTURE buffer.
  VIDIOC_SUBSCRIBE_EVENT     V4L2_EVENT_EOS and V4L2_EVENT_CTRL. Both exist on
  VIDIOC_UNSUBSCRIBE_EVENT   this tree; without ENCODER_CMD there is nothing
  VIDIOC_DQEVENT             to signal yet. v4l2_m2m_poll() on this tree does
                             not report POLLPRI, so event delivery needs a
                             poll wrapper as well as the three ioctls.
  VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS         vb2_create_bufs() exists on this tree; not wired.
  VIDIOC_PREPARE_BUF         vb2_prepare_buf() exists; not wired.
  VIDIOC_G_CROP / S_CROP /
    CROPCAP                  no cropping is offered; the visible rectangle is
                             the format.
  VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES     a client cannot discover the 16-pixel width step
    / ENUM_FRAMEINTERVALS    or the 1280 limit without TRY_FMT.
  V4L2_BUF_FLAG_LAST         absent from this kernel; a modern client keys its
                             drain on it and will not see one.
  VIDIOC_EXPBUF,
    V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF       absent from this kernel's V4L2 core; USERPTR is
                             the zero-copy path here. Settled, not a gap to
                             reopen.

2. Controls
-----------

Standard, all with both the define and a case in
drivers/media/video/v4l2-ctrls.c on this tree:

  H264_I_FRAME_QP, H264_P_FRAME_QP, H264_MIN_QP, H264_MAX_QP
  BITRATE, BITRATE_MODE (VBR/CBR), GOP_SIZE, VBV_SIZE (the CPB, kilobytes)
  FRAME_RC_ENABLE (default off), MB_RC_ENABLE (default on)
  H264_PROFILE (Baseline and Constrained Baseline only), H264_LEVEL

Private, at V4L2_CID_USER_BASE + 0x1100, for the tuning with no standard CID:

  +0 H264 Intra QP Delta          the I frame's offset from the P QP
  +1 H264 I Frame Budget Weight   the GOP budget split
  +2 MB Rate Control Mode         MAD or Checkpoint - they are alternatives
  +3 MAD Based QP Adjustment      signed, -8..7
  +4 MAD Threshold                0..63, the seed of the adaptation loop
  +5 MAD Threshold Adaptation

Not exposed: the motion-estimation penalties and mode favours. They are a
function of QP the driver owes the hardware, and a user-settable value would
break the rate controller quietly. V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_CYCLIC_INTRA_REFRESH_MB
stays unexposed too: the recovered register map has no cyclic-refresh field.
Entropy mode stays unexposed until the CABAC context table generator exists,
which is why H264_PROFILE offers no Main.

Force-key-frame: V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_FORCE_KEY_FRAME does NOT exist in this
tree, and its upstream number is not in this tree either, so it is neither used
nor invented. The driver exposes V4L2_CID_MPEG_MFC51_VIDEO_FORCE_FRAME_TYPE,
which is defined in include/linux/videodev2.h here but has no case in
v4l2-ctrls.c - so it is registered with v4l2_ctrl_new_custom() and the driver
supplies the name and the menu. Backporting the standard CID (a define plus one
case, with the number taken from a current upstream v4l2-controls.h) is the
right fix and is not done here.

Still missing and still a gap: HEADER_MODE, ENTROPY_MODE, MULTI_SLICE_MODE,
the loop-filter controls and the JPEG class.

3. Stage-two behaviour that is not the final behaviour
------------------------------------------------------

- The rate controller runs in the interrupt handler rather than in a threaded
  handler. It is bounded integer arithmetic with no 64-bit division, so it is
  affordable there, but the design asks for request_threaded_irq().
- A control write lands in the context's rate-control config without a lock and
  is picked up by the next device_run. Each field is word sized, so a write can
  be missed by one frame but not torn.
- Deblocking, chroma QP offset and slice size are fixed at the values the first
  hardware encode used; only QP-dependent registers move per frame.
- The MAD stage now runs the designed values (-2, threshold 12, adapting)
  rather than stage one's frozen vendor pair (-3, threshold 8). To reproduce
  the stage-one bitstream exactly, set MB_RC_ENABLE=1, MB Rate Control Mode to
  MAD, MAD Based QP Adjustment to -3, MAD Threshold to 8, MAD Threshold
  Adaptation to 0, FRAME_RC_ENABLE=0, QP 30, and do not call S_PARM.
  workspace/capture/vencstream.c has a flag for each of those.
- ak-venc-rc.c is duplicated as lib/video/ak-venc-rc.c in the firmware tree,
  byte for byte, so that ak-venc-rc_test can run on the host. Task 4 folds the
  copies together.
- ak-venc-hw.c, ak-venc-h264.c and ak-venc-regs.h are stage-one copies of
  lib/video/akcam_h8290.c, akcam_bits.c + akcam_h264_hdr.c and
  akcam_h8290_fields.h. Task 4 replaces them with the shared sources so the
  host test that replays the vendor register captures runs against the same
  code the driver compiles. Until then the two copies can drift.
- The NAL length table is over-allocated at one 32-bit entry per macroblock row
  plus sixteen. The exact figure the core needs is not established; a bigger
  table is harmless, a smaller one corrupts what follows it.
- Only I420 is enumerated on OUTPUT. The core also takes NV12, YUYV and UYVY,
  but the capture path delivers I420 and the ISP driver already enumerates an
  NV12 it does not produce.

4. Open questions this stage cannot settle
-------------------------------------------

- The frame-store slot swap cadence is inferred from two slots and IPPP; it has
  never been observed. Ten frames of a static scene decode without drift only
  if it is right.
- The core writes its own four-byte start code for the slice NAL, so an inter
  frame has nothing prepended and the stream base is the buffer itself. That
  follows from the stage-one encode, where the software header was not followed
  by a start code and ffmpeg decoded the result; it has not been seen for a
  frame with no software header at all.
- Whether a checkpoint fires, where it lands, and the sign convention of the
  error ladder. Nothing in either vendor capture exercises the stage.
- Whether MAD_BASED_QP_ADJUSTMENT applies below the threshold, and whether the
  adaptation loop converges to a third of the picture rather than sitting at a
  rail.
- What bitrate the frame-level loop actually achieves.
- Coexistence with drivers/uio/uio_video_codec.c is not safe, only convenient.
  Both drivers take interrupt 2 on demand - UIO on its first open, this driver
  on its first STREAMON - so whichever is first wins and the other fails. But
  uio_vcodec_open() ignores the return of request_irq() and then calls
  disable_irq_nosync(2) unconditionally, so opening /dev/uio0 while this driver
  is streaming silently disables the line this driver is waiting on, and
  uio_vcodec_release() then free_irq()s a cookie it does not own. Making the
  two Kconfig entries mutually exclusive, and dropping the UIO device from the
  board file once this driver is the production path, is the real fix. Note
  also that probe() pulses the clock and reset to read the ASIC id, so loading
  this as a module while something else is mid-encode resets the core under it.

- One CPU mapping of the CAPTURE buffer is needed to write SPS and PPS into it.
  MMAP buffers have a kernel address; USERPTR buffers do not on this tree, so
  buf_prepare ioremaps them. An ION carveout mapped write-combining in
  userspace and ioremap_nocache here is a mismatched-attribute alias - both are
  non-cacheable, but it should be checked once on hardware.

5. What only hardware can settle, in the order to try it
---------------------------------------------------------

Each item is one run of workspace/capture/vencstream.c against a stored I420
frame. Keep runs short: capture and encode wedge this board at a rate that
climbs with run length.

1. Ten frames, fixed QP 30, GOP 10, static input:
     vencstream in.yuv out.264 -n 10 -g 10 -q 30 -p 30
   One IDR then nine P frames. The P frames of a static scene should be a small
   fraction of the IDR. Decode with ffmpeg and check for drift: that is the
   reference-rotation cadence, and a frozen rotation drifts visibly.
2. The same run with -k 5, which forces an IDR mid-stream through
   FORCE_FRAME_TYPE. Two IDRs, no drift across the second.
3. The stage-one reproduction, which must be byte-identical to the frame the
   gate produced: -n 1 -q 30 -m 1 -M mad -D -3 -t 8 -a 0, no -b, no -f.
4. Frame-level rate control: -n 50 -g 25 -b 2000000 -f 25. Check the mean rate
   is within 5% of the target and that no frame exceeds four times its budget.
5. The MAD adaptation loop: -m 1 -M mad over a hundred frames, watching that
   the qualifying fraction moves toward a third rather than sitting at a rail.
6. The checkpoint stage: -m 1 -M checkpoint. Before trusting it, run the
   calibration of the rate-control design's section 12.1 - one checkpoint at
   the halfway macroblock, all seven deltas at +7 - because nothing in either
   vendor capture exercises this stage.
7. An error frame: a deliberately small CAPTURE buffer to raise buffer-full,
   and check the next frame comes back as an IDR and that the stream after it
   still decodes.

6. JPEG
-------

V4L2_PIX_FMT_JPEG on the CAPTURE queue, chosen per frame, with
V4L2_CID_JPEG_COMPRESSION_QUALITY as its only knob. The picture size still
comes from S_FMT on the OUTPUT queue, as it does for H.264.

What the core does and what software does:

  the core        the entropy-coded scan, and the EOI marker - swreg20 bit 24
                  is "JPEG slice enable. 0=picture ends with EOI", and slices
                  are disabled. Software must not append one.
  software        SOI, APP0, DQT, SOF0, DHT, SOS, written into the head of the
                  CAPTURE buffer. The core continues from the last whole 64-bit
                  word of that header, so the finished file is the buffer from
                  offset zero and nothing is spliced afterwards.

The Huffman tables are not negotiable: the core has no register for them, so
the entropy coder is wired to the standard baseline tables of T.81 Annex K and
the DHT has to say so. The quantisation tables ARE registers - swreg64..95,
four 8-bit entries each, in a load order that is neither raster nor zigzag -
and they are the only thing quality changes.

Stateless, which is the property the rest of the system depends on:

  - No reference frame, no reconstruction frame, no NAL length table, no CABAC
    block. ak_venc_alloc_priv() allocates none of them for a JPEG context.
  - The interrupt handler does not feed the rate controller, rotate the
    reference stores, advance the GOP or touch frame_num for a JPEG frame, and
    a failed JPEG does not force the next H.264 frame intra.
  - The register file is rebuilt from zero for each JPEG frame, so no H.264
    field survives into it.

So a snapshot costs a running H.264 session one frame of core time and nothing
else. Two contexts - one H.264, one JPEG - interleave safely, because each
device_run rewrites swreg1..0x3e in full from its own shadow.

PROVEN before hardware: the field semantics, all of them quoted from the
vendor's own descriptor table; that JPEG allocates nothing; that the software
half is correct, because splicing lib/video's header in front of libjpeg's scan
for the same picture and the same tables decodes pixel-identical to libjpeg's
own file.

INFERRED and needing the board: that the core accepts a mid-word handover for
JPEG as it does for H.264 (same three registers, same arithmetic, but only the
H.264 side has run); that the quantisation load order is what the vendor's
permutation says it is; that swreg24 reads back in bits for JPEG as it does for
H.264; that the core does not touch the frame stores in JPEG mode, which is
what the vendor's zeroed address registers imply.

The gate, in order:

8. One 1280x720 JPEG from a stored I420 frame through openjpg - the userspace
   proof, which is what settles all four inferences above at once. A host
   decoder must open it at 1280x720 and the picture must look like the input.
   Sweep the quality control: 20, 50, 80, 95, and check the file size moves
   monotonically and every one decodes.
9. The same frame through this driver at the same quality, byte-compared
   against openjpg's output. Anything but equality is a difference in the
   register programming or the header, and the diff says which.
10. A JPEG frame taken from a second context while an H.264 session is
    streaming: the H.264 stream either side of it must decode with no drift and
    no dropped frame, which is what proves the interleave.
